## Why This Is Asked

This validates practical isolation in a shared data lake, ensuring least privilege and scalable onboarding. It also tests policy layering and auditing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- IAM roles per tenant with strict resource boundaries
- Trust policies tied to the tenant app identity
- S3 prefix-scoped permissions with bucket or IAM policy conditions
- Onboarding automation for new tenants and centralized governance
- Cross-tenant test plans and auditing hooks

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-<tid>/*"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake/tenant-<tid>/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/TenantRole-<tid>",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this model to support cross-region backups without cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you automate onboarding for new tenants without touching code (CI/CD policy issuance, audit log integration)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account access, tenant isolation, and end-to-end data export controls in a realistic setup.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account roles and STS AssumeRole with session tags
- S3 prefixes and object-level permissions
- TLS enforcement and per-tenant KMS keys
- CloudTrail auditing of cross-account activity

## Code Example

```json
// Trust policy for the tenant-scoped ExportRole
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA-LAKE-ACCOUNT:role/ExporterRole" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "aws:RequestTag/tenant_id": "tenant-123" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Tenant bucket policy sample for restricted CopyObject
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject","s3:PutObjectAcl","s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket-123","arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket-123/*"],
      "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption": "aws:kms" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to dozens/hundreds of tenants?
- What are failure modes if the tenant account lacks KMS keys or if the ExportRole permissions are too permissive?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical understanding of serverless data ingestion on AWS, IAM least privilege, and TLS enforcement.

## Key Concepts

- TLS endpoint
- IAM roles and trust policies for Lambda
- S3 bucket policy scoped to path and principal
- Simple integration test with canaries

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda writes payload to S3
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const body = event.body || JSON.stringify(event);
  const date = new Date().toISOString().substring(0,10);
  const key = `logs/${date}/payload.json`;
  await s3.putObject({Bucket: process.env.BUCKET, Key: key, Body: body, ContentType: 'application/json'}).promise();
  return {statusCode: 200, body: 'ok'};
};
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::data-ingest", "arn:aws:s3:::data-ingest/*"],
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<acct>:role/<lambda-execution-role>"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-ingest/logs/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate the Lambda execution role credentials without downtime?
- How would you add basic telemetry for failed writes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end AWS streaming design, data locality, replay semantics, and observability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality, replay, idempotency, cross-region replication, shard throttling, and cost trade-offs.
- Durability vs latency in KDS/Kafka vs Firehose patterns.
- Observability via CloudWatch, X-Ray, and structured metrics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent sink to DynamoDB
async function sinkEvent(event){
  const key = event.tenantId + '#' + event.id;
  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'TenantEvents',
    Item: { PK: key, ...event },
    ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(PK)'
  }).catch(err => {
    if (err.code === 'ConditionalCheckFailedException') return; // idempotent retry
    throw err
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle shard throttling and bursty traffic?
- How would you onboard new tenants without impacting latency or isolation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cross-account IAM, KMS usage, and end-to-end data flow in a real-world streaming pipeline, plus a concrete test plan.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account trust with IAM roles and principals
- Kinesis PutRecords permissions and TLS enforcement
- SSE-KMS with a CMK in the data-owner account
- Lambda assuming a restricted role using session tags for fine-grained access
- Test strategy: canaries, revocation timing, and anomaly detection

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ProducerRole"},
      "Action": "kinesis:PutRecords",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:REGION:ACCOUNT_B:stream/STREAM_NAME",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/partner": "Acme"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey*"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT_B:key/CMK_ID",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"kms:ViaService": "kinesis.REGION.amazonaws.com"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate the CMK with minimal disruption?
- How would you monitor PutRecords failures and automatically alert on unauthorized access attempts?

